It's more than just the words.

78
00:04:23,690 --> 00:04:24,440
OK, moving on.

79
00:04:25,100 --> 00:04:27,540
Politeness and collaboration.

80
00:04:27,580 --> 00:04:28,630
Super important.

81
00:04:28,650 --> 00:04:31,410
Phrase number two is incredibly useful.

82
00:04:32,100 --> 00:04:33,120
Almost essential, really.

83
00:04:33,720 --> 00:04:37,410
When you have a minute or maybe do you have a minute?

84
00:04:37,390 --> 00:04:37,930
Yes.

85
00:04:38,330 --> 00:04:50,480
This is an extremely polite way, a very considerate way to ask for someone's time or maybe their help with something, especially when, you know, or you just assume they might be busy or focused on something else.

86
00:04:51,080 --> 00:04:54,200
It automatically shows respect for their work, their schedule.

87
00:04:54,380 --> 00:04:55,610
Instead of just barging in.

88
00:04:55,630 --> 00:04:56,130
Exactly.

89
00:04:56,190 --> 00:05:02,570
Instead of being abrupt or demanding, you're giving them the courtesy, the choice to respond when they actually can give it their attention.

90
00:05:02,650 --> 00:05:04,920
It respects their control over their own time.

91
00:05:04,890 --> 00:05:11,590
So rather than just walking up or pinging someone, look at this now, you'd say maybe, hey, when you have a minute, could you glance at this report for me?

92
00:05:11,750 --> 00:05:12,070
Perfect.

93
00:05:12,170 --> 00:05:14,450
Or maybe a bit more immediate, but still polite.

94
00:05:14,890 --> 00:05:17,420
Do you have a minute to look at this report?

95
00:05:17,440 --> 00:05:19,220
It just smooths things over, doesn't it?

96
00:05:19,620 --> 00:05:20,860
Shows you see they're busy.

97
00:05:20,910 --> 00:05:22,190
It absolutely does.

98
00:05:22,270 --> 00:05:25,190
That phrasing subtly says, I see you're working.

99
00:05:25,470 --> 00:05:26,440
I respect your time.

100
00:05:27,080 --> 00:05:33,660
It emphasizes that respect, which builds better working relationships based on consideration, not just demands.

101
00:05:34,280 --> 00:05:38,650
For anyone working on their business English, mastering these polite phrases is huge.

102
00:05:39,170 --> 00:05:40,150
It's not just grammar.

103
00:05:40,230 --> 00:05:46,920
It's like showing emotional intelligence in how you communicate, anticipating friction and smoothing it over.

104
00:05:46,980 --> 00:05:49,020
That makes you a better collaborator.

105
00:05:49,360 --> 00:05:50,560
Hugely valuable.

106
00:05:50,540 --> 00:05:54,000
OK, phrase number three paints a really vivid picture.

107
00:05:54,020 --> 00:05:58,390
It's kind of playful to bounce some ideas off of you, sometimes just off you.

108
00:05:58,590 --> 00:06:00,570
What was bouncing ideas really mean here?

109
00:06:00,950 --> 00:06:03,430
Sounds a bit like you're throwing a ball at someone.

110
00:06:04,240 --> 00:06:04,940
It does, doesn't it?

111
00:06:05,080 --> 00:06:08,040
And that highlights how informal but effective it is.

112
00:06:08,440 --> 00:06:09,480
It's pretty much what it sounds like.

113
00:06:09,520 --> 00:06:14,400
You're sharing an idea, maybe one that's not fully formed yet, just to get someone's quick, informal reaction.

114
00:06:14,930 --> 00:06:16,350
OK, so not a formal pitch.

115
00:06:16,530 --> 00:06:17,150
Not at all.

116
00:06:17,470 --> 00:06:23,020
The key thing is there's zero expectation of getting solid, finished answers back.

117
00:06:23,040 --> 00:06:26,350
The goal is more like collaborative refining.

118
00:06:26,730 --> 00:06:32,400
You toss the idea out, see how it lands, get that gut reaction, and that feedback helps you make it better together.

119
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:38,050
Or maybe realize it needs rethinking, like a mini brainstorm, just two people.

120
00:06:38,030 --> 00:06:41,210
So you're not asking for the final answer, just testing the waters.

121
00:06:41,450 --> 00:06:41,790
Yeah.

122
00:06:41,810 --> 00:06:42,860
Like, hey, want to grab coffee?

123
00:06:43,180 --> 00:06:45,360
I'd like to bounce some ideas off you for this new assignment.

124
00:06:45,400 --> 00:06:46,100
Just see what you think.

125
00:06:46,280 --> 00:06:46,860
Exactly.

126
00:06:46,880 --> 00:06:52,010
Or maybe I've been mulling over this project strategy and I'd love to bounce a few ideas off you.

127
00:06:52,050 --> 00:06:52,850
Get a couple of minutes.

128
00:06:53,530 --> 00:06:55,510
It shows you value their first impressions.

129
00:06:55,730 --> 00:07:00,890
And it connects directly to that whole process of creative problem solving, innovation.

130
00:07:01,680 --> 00:07:05,340
This phrase is great for encouraging open, low pressure discussion.

131
00:07:05,960 --> 00:07:10,660
It gets you that quick, informal feedback that can shape an idea before you invest too much time in it.

132
00:07:11,120 --> 00:07:16,450
What's fascinating is how it promotes that iterative thinking, allows for quick adjustments.

133
00:07:16,790 --> 00:07:24,240
For anyone looking to improve their business English, using this phrase shows you're open to collaboration, willing to ask for input.

134
00:07:24,210 --> 00:07:26,200
Those are really valued traits.

135
00:07:26,220 --> 00:07:30,550
Plus, it shows you can start a dynamic conversation, not just a transactional one.

136
00:07:31,210 --> 00:07:33,940
OK. And speaking of getting input, phrase number five.

137
00:07:34,540 --> 00:07:36,670
This one always sounds a little strange to me.

138
00:07:37,330 --> 00:07:38,330
To pick your brains.

139
00:07:39,070 --> 00:07:42,140
A bit Frankenstein-ish, right?

140
00:07:42,120 --> 00:07:44,930
Yeah, the imagery is a bit odd.

141
00:07:44,950 --> 00:07:48,860
But here's where it gets really interesting because it's so common and useful.

142
00:07:49,380 --> 00:07:51,200
But is there like an etiquette to it?

143
00:07:51,260 --> 00:07:55,120
Can you just randomly ask to pick someone's brain or should it be more planned?

144
00:07:55,260 --> 00:07:56,690
That's a really good question.

145
00:07:56,750 --> 00:07:58,970
There's definitely an unspoken etiquette.

146
00:07:59,410 --> 00:08:06,920
While it sounds literal to pick your brains, just means asking for input or advice or insights from someone who knows more about something than you do.

147
00:08:07,440 --> 00:08:09,710
Someone with more experience or specific knowledge.

148
00:08:10,230 --> 00:08:12,890
It's actually a respectful way to acknowledge their expertise.

149
00:08:12,910 --> 00:08:14,840
You're seeking wisdom, not making demands.

150
00:08:15,260 --> 00:08:19,800
And no, you definitely can't just demand someone's time or knowledge without being considerate.

151
00:08:19,870 --> 00:08:20,170
Right.

152
00:08:20,250 --> 00:08:26,400
So you might say, I know you've been here five years and really get the company culture, so I'd love to pick your brains about what it's really like to work here.

153
00:08:26,980 --> 00:08:27,760
Perfect example.

154
00:08:28,020 --> 00:08:36,880
Or maybe with your background in market analysis, could we maybe hop on a call so I can pick your brains about the strategy for next quarter?

155
00:08:37,770 --> 00:08:39,560
It's about learning, but respectfully.

156
00:08:39,800 --> 00:08:40,360
Exactly.

157
00:08:40,380 --> 00:08:42,460
And a good brain picking session.

158
00:08:42,480 --> 00:08:48,630
That's one where you come prepared with specific questions, respect their time, be focused.

159
00:08:48,650 --> 00:08:55,210
Ideally, you're also thinking about how you might return the favor later or at least follow up to show how their advice helped.

160
00:08:55,270 --> 00:08:56,790
So it's more like building a connection.

161
00:08:56,860 --> 00:09:03,160
Yes, less about just extracting information, more about building a relationship, maybe even mentorship.

162
00:09:03,870 --> 00:09:10,620
Using this phrase well for improving your business English signals professionalism, humility, and that you're proactive about learning.

163
00:09:11,040 --> 00:09:14,340
Shows you know how to access knowledge, which is a great soft skill.

164
00:09:14,480 --> 00:09:17,080
Let's shift to efficient information exchange.

165
00:09:17,380 --> 00:09:19,670
Speed, clarity, super important today.

166
00:09:20,290 --> 00:09:23,380
Phrase number four, which we skipped over, is first thing in the morning.

167
00:09:24,260 --> 00:09:28,570
I notice people sometimes add the first thing in the morning, but it's more direct, right?

168
00:09:28,590 --> 00:09:31,120
And what does morning mean with time zones everywhere?

169
00:09:31,220 --> 00:09:31,960
Great points.

170
00:09:32,720 --> 00:09:34,860
Yes, it is a fixed phrase, no the.

171
00:09:34,900 --> 00:09:39,770
It means doing something right away at the very start of the workday.

172
00:09:39,810 --> 00:09:45,180
It's understood as an immediate block of time, sets a clear expectation for prompt action.

173
00:09:45,870 --> 00:09:49,070
But you're right, the time zone thing is a real issue in global teams.

174
00:09:49,170 --> 00:09:51,860
So you could say, like, first thing in the morning I make coffee.

175
00:09:52,380 --> 00:09:52,800
A routine.

176
00:09:53,580 --> 00:09:54,280
Or work-wise.

177
00:09:54,700 --> 00:09:57,400
If I don't hear from the client today, I'll call them first thing in the morning.

178
00:09:57,860 --> 00:09:59,650
It's a commitment for the start of the next day.

179
00:09:59,890 --> 00:10:01,030
Or maybe finishing up late.

180
00:10:01,450 --> 00:10:03,910
Sorry, can't finish the report now, but I'll do it first thing in the morning.

181
00:10:04,070 --> 00:10:06,920
Super useful locally, but globally.

182
00:10:06,940 --> 00:10:08,680
Yeah, first thing can cause confusion.

183
00:10:08,980 --> 00:10:11,990
Best practice often is to add the time zone.

184
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:14,970
First thing ET or first thing GMT.

185
00:10:15,350 --> 00:10:16,190
Oh, that makes sense.

186
00:10:16,170 --> 00:10:20,040
Otherwise, your first thing might be their middle of the night leads to problems.

187
00:10:21,720 --> 00:10:29,660
So for improving business English in an international setting, being explicit about time zones shows real communicative competence.

188
00:10:29,980 --> 00:10:32,970
It's practical application, not just knowing the phrase.

189
00:10:33,280 --> 00:10:37,030
Keeping with efficiency, phrase six is huge now with remote work.

190
00:10:37,710 --> 00:10:38,750
To hop on a call.

191
00:10:39,630 --> 00:10:42,280
Sounds casual like hopping on a bus, but it's totally professional.

192
00:10:42,300 --> 00:10:42,740
It does.

193
00:10:42,860 --> 00:10:45,180
And that casual feel is part of its usefulness.

194
00:10:45,730 --> 00:10:49,010
To hop on a call means starting a conversation.

195
00:10:48,990 --> 00:10:53,140
often pretty spontaneously using the phone or Zoom, Teams, whatever.

196
00:10:53,840 --> 00:11:01,940
It suggests a quick direct chat about something that's maybe easier or faster to sort out by talking in real time rather than endless emails back and forth.

197
00:11:02,440 --> 00:11:03,370
Implies immediacy.

198
00:11:03,780 --> 00:11:03,970
Right.

199
00:11:04,050 --> 00:11:08,050
So if a client has urgent questions, you might ask a colleague, hey, get a minute to hop on a call.

200
00:11:08,330 --> 00:11:09,770
Client just sent some urgent questions.

201
00:11:09,790 --> 00:11:10,370
Perfect.

202
00:11:10,390 --> 00:11:16,560
Or if an email thread is getting way too complicated, you might say, I was going to email again, but maybe it's just easier if we talk.

203
00:11:16,580 --> 00:11:17,640
Can we hop on a call real quick?

204
00:11:17,980 --> 00:11:18,900
Kind of through the back and forth.

205
00:11:19,030 --> 00:11:29,500
It's a strong signal that you prefer real time discussion over asynchronous stuff like email, especially for urgent things or complex issues where tone matters.

206
00:11:29,880 --> 00:11:31,650
Yeah, nuance gets lost in text sometimes.

207
00:11:31,670 --> 00:11:32,270
Exactly.

208
00:11:32,550 --> 00:11:39,420
It shows you want immediate resolution or clarity, makes communication more dynamic, often way more effective.

209
00:11:39,400 --> 00:11:44,920
Using to hop on a call, well, for improving your business English shows you're flexible.

210
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:49,270
You understand dynamic communication needs, shows you're ready for direct engagement.

211
00:11:49,510 --> 00:11:56,660
And speaking of quick and direct, phrase seven, another two for one, to shoot off an email or to fire off an email.

212
00:11:57,380 --> 00:11:59,700
Both shoot and fire sound fast, right?

213
00:12:00,240 --> 00:12:01,100
Intentional.

214
00:12:01,120 --> 00:12:01,760
They really do.

215
00:12:01,880 --> 00:12:04,350
Both mean sending an email or maybe even a text quickly.

216
00:12:04,490 --> 00:12:08,170
The emphasis is on speed, efficiency, maybe conciseness.

217
00:12:08,290 --> 00:12:10,310
So not a long, detailed message.

218
00:12:10,330 --> 00:12:11,040
Probably not.

219
00:12:11,180 --> 00:12:16,490
It implies you're sending the essential info rapidly without lots of extra stuff, just dispatching the message.

220
00:12:16,650 --> 00:12:16,950
Got it.

221
00:12:17,400 --> 00:12:21,170
So I'm going to fire off an email with these key questions before our client meeting.

222
00:12:21,200 --> 00:12:22,780
or waiting for info.

223
00:12:23,140 --> 00:12:26,390
Just shoot me an email with those numbers and I'll tackle it first thing in the morning.

224
00:12:26,630 --> 00:12:26,810
See?

225
00:12:26,870 --> 00:12:28,090
They combine.

226
00:12:28,250 --> 00:12:28,930
Exactly.

227
00:12:28,950 --> 00:12:30,570
You're combining them perfectly.

228
00:12:30,610 --> 00:12:36,360
That shows how these phrases link together for efficient, natural business communication.

229
00:12:37,380 --> 00:12:47,020
Understanding the urgency in shoot-off or fire-off for improving your business English helps you prioritize and communicate expectations clearly.

230
00:12:47,130 --> 00:12:49,280
Direct, but still professional.

231
00:12:50,040 --> 00:12:51,090
Streamlines things.

232
00:12:51,350 --> 00:12:53,070
Okay, last one in this section, number eight.

233
00:12:53,450 --> 00:12:54,150
Great visual.

234
00:12:54,570 --> 00:12:55,900
To keep someone in the loop.

235
00:12:56,600 --> 00:12:59,830
A loop makes you think of connection, inclusion, information flow.

236
00:12:59,910 --> 00:13:00,490
Precisely.

237
00:13:00,710 --> 00:13:08,440
To keep someone in the loop means actively making sure that person is included, that they get all the necessary updates about a project, a discussion, whatever's going on.

238
00:13:08,480 --> 00:13:09,200
So they're not left out.

239
00:13:09,460 --> 00:13:09,700
Right.

240
00:13:09,800 --> 00:13:12,400
Making sure they're fully informed, not operating with old info.

241
00:13:12,610 --> 00:13:17,090
So if you missed a meeting, you might ask someone, hey, missed the planning meeting yesterday.

242
00:13:17,170 --> 00:13:18,950
Could you keep me in the loop on the decision?

243
00:13:19,030 --> 00:13:20,150
Send me the notes and stuff.

244
00:13:20,170 --> 00:13:21,160
Perfect use.

245
00:13:21,180 --> 00:13:23,620
Or maybe a boss needs updates on a big project.

246
00:13:24,340 --> 00:13:29,110
The VP wants you to keep her in the loop on all client comms for this initiative.

247
00:13:29,240 --> 00:13:38,280
It's so important for team collaboration, for transparency, for effective information flow, avoids miscommunication, makes sure everyone's on the same page.

248
00:13:38,760 --> 00:13:42,860
And honestly, it prevents people from feeling isolated or undervalued.

249
00:13:43,020 --> 00:13:44,260
Yeah, that's huge for morale.

250
00:13:44,240 --> 00:13:44,900
Absolutely.

251
00:13:45,060 --> 00:13:48,290
So actively using phrases like keep me in the loop.

252
00:13:48,630 --> 00:13:51,770
If you're working on your business, English shows you understand team dynamics.

253
00:13:51,860 --> 00:13:55,720
You're committed to collaboration and you know how to manage information sharing.

254
00:13:56,120 --> 00:13:59,590
It's about building that cohesive, informed team.

255
00:14:00,270 --> 00:14:01,250
Fundamental stuff.

256
00:14:01,760 --> 00:14:12,470
OK, let's shift gears a bit, moving from those daily interaction phrases to the world of strategic thinking and project management, where innovation and execution are really the name of the game.

257
00:14:12,510 --> 00:14:14,340
Phrase number nine is a big one for creative work.

258
00:14:15,260 --> 00:14:16,020
To brainstorm.

259
00:14:16,400 --> 00:14:17,320
You can almost picture it, right?

260
00:14:17,420 --> 00:14:18,740
A meeting room, ideas flying.

261
00:14:18,880 --> 00:14:19,360
Totally.

262
00:14:19,420 --> 00:14:23,090
To brainstorm is all about generating lots of new ideas, usually in a group.

263
00:14:23,370 --> 00:14:25,190
The main goal is to get all the ideas out there.

264
00:14:25,270 --> 00:14:26,410
No bad ideas at first.

265
00:14:26,390 --> 00:14:27,030
Exactly.

266
00:14:27,050 --> 00:14:28,520
You suspend judgment initially.

267
00:14:28,560 --> 00:14:30,020
Doesn't matter how wild it sounds.

268
00:14:30,060 --> 00:14:31,340
You want a free flow of thoughts.

269
00:14:31,960 --> 00:14:33,680
Quantity over immediate quality.

270
00:14:33,780 --> 00:14:39,170
Because somewhere in that mass of ideas, you'll find something that works or sparks something even better.

271
00:14:39,370 --> 00:14:39,830
Makes sense.

272
00:14:40,070 --> 00:14:41,510
Get it all out first, then filter.

273
00:14:42,130 --> 00:14:47,900
So a team lead might say, OK, team, tomorrow's meeting, we're going to brainstorm new marketing ideas for Q3.

274
00:14:47,980 --> 00:14:48,780
Come with everything.

275
00:14:49,340 --> 00:14:54,530
Or our group got together specifically to brainstorm better ways to help customers with the new website.

276
00:14:54,790 --> 00:14:58,220
And good brainstorming, effective brainstorming, it's more than just listing ideas.

277
00:14:58,480 --> 00:14:59,540
It's about the environment.

278
00:15:00,080 --> 00:15:03,500
Is it safe for people to share truly out there ideas?

279
00:15:03,940 --> 00:15:08,070
Are people building on each other's thoughts or just waiting for their turn to talk?

280
00:15:08,990 --> 00:15:14,840
Modern brainstorming might use specific techniques too, like reverse brainstorming, focusing on problems first.

281
00:15:15,340 --> 00:15:17,040
Structured methods like Scamper.

282
00:15:17,620 --> 00:15:23,800
It pushes for diverse thinking, moves beyond just raw ideas into structured innovation.

283
00:15:23,780 --> 00:15:32,070
Participating well in brainstorming for improving your business English shows you're collaborative, innovative, a key strategic skill.

284
00:15:32,150 --> 00:15:38,400
Now, after something happens or a project finishes, phrase 10 is key for learning.

285
00:15:39,300 --> 00:15:44,250
To debrief sounds a bit formal, maybe military, like reporting back.

286
00:15:44,370 --> 00:15:45,230
That's a good analogy.

287
00:15:45,530 --> 00:15:46,390
The idea is similar.

288
00:15:46,710 --> 00:15:53,900
To debrief means systematically summarizing, giving a detailed account after an event or project, usually in a structured meeting.

289
00:15:54,060 --> 00:15:54,700
What's the goal?

290
00:15:54,680 --> 00:16:00,310
to discuss what happened, what went well, what didn't, and crucially, how to improve next time.

291
00:16:00,350 --> 00:16:05,070
It works as a verb, let's debrief, or a noun, we need a debrief.

292
00:16:05,160 --> 00:16:05,500
Got it.

293
00:16:05,560 --> 00:16:11,190
So after a big product launch, you might say, OK, now that it's launched, let's schedule a meeting to debrief next week.

294
00:16:11,690 --> 00:16:15,020
Or if it already happened, we had a thorough debrief.

295
00:16:15,040 --> 00:16:18,040
And based on that, we're making some changes for the next project cycle.

296
00:16:18,480 --> 00:16:20,050
It's really about learning from what you just did.

297
00:16:20,210 --> 00:16:20,710
Absolutely.

298
00:16:20,810 --> 00:16:24,690
And connecting this to the bigger picture, debriefing isn't just important.

299
00:16:24,820 --> 00:16:27,360
It's essential for real organizational growth.

300
00:16:27,440 --> 00:16:28,200
Why essential?

301
00:16:28,180 --> 00:16:31,620
Because its real power is in creating psychological safety.

302
00:16:31,980 --> 00:16:35,710
Too often, teams rush it, or worse, it becomes about blaming people.

303
00:16:35,910 --> 00:16:36,710
Yeah, I've seen that.

304
00:16:36,730 --> 00:16:40,790
But a good debrief focuses on systems, not just individuals.

305
00:16:41,430 --> 00:16:46,120
It encourages people to be open, reframes failures as learning moments for everyone.

306
00:16:46,540 --> 00:16:48,540
It builds that continuous improvement loop.

307
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:56,330
Participating effectively in a debrief for improving business English shows leadership potential, commitment to growth.

308
00:16:56,310 --> 00:16:59,240
It's sophisticated project management understanding.

309
00:16:59,840 --> 00:16:59,940
Okay.

310
00:16:59,960 --> 00:17:03,520
Now, for a couple of phrases that describe work ethic, often in contrast.

311
00:17:04,200 --> 00:17:07,530
Phrase 11, nobody wants to be called this, a slacker.

312
00:17:08,570 --> 00:17:09,990
It's unfortunately a thing sometimes.

313
00:17:10,110 --> 00:17:11,560
No, definitely not a label you want.

314
00:17:11,580 --> 00:17:16,400
A slacker is someone who's, well, lazy, doesn't do their fair share, avoids responsibility.

315
00:17:16,440 --> 00:17:18,710
And expects others to pick up the, well, slack.

316
00:17:18,690 --> 00:17:19,590
Often, yeah.

317
00:17:19,610 --> 00:17:26,760
Implicitly or explicitly, they expect others to cover for them, which is incredibly frustrating, bad for morale, and obviously bad for getting the work done well.

318
00:17:26,960 --> 00:17:33,570
You hear people complaining, maybe quietly, he's such a slacker, always late, and the rest of us have to scramble.

319
00:17:34,270 --> 00:17:40,560
Or thinking back to group projects, it seems like every group has one slacker who expects everyone else to do the heavy lifting.

320
00:17:41,120 --> 00:17:42,420
It really drags a team down.

321
00:17:42,480 --> 00:17:43,260
It really does.

322
00:17:43,320 --> 00:17:47,030
The phrase highlights that frustration, the negative impact on morale.

323
00:17:47,870 --> 00:17:49,850
Creates resentment when the workload isn't fair.

324
00:17:50,430 --> 00:17:54,110
It's the exact opposite of the reliability and commitment everyone values.

325
00:17:55,250 --> 00:18:02,320
Understanding the weight of this term, even if you don't use it, helps you recognize unhealthy team dynamics when improving your business English.

326
00:18:02,660 --> 00:18:08,710
So if slacker is the negative pole, phrase 12 is the positive opposite, a workhorse is

327
00:18:08,770 --> 00:18:09,830
Now, that's high praise.

328
00:18:10,170 --> 00:18:10,780
Huge praise.

329
00:18:11,220 --> 00:18:17,370
A workhorse is someone super diligent, reliable, works incredibly hard, often has great endurance.

330
00:18:17,830 --> 00:18:22,320
They just consistently get stuff done, often going the extra mile without needing to be pushed.

331
00:18:22,860 --> 00:18:24,100
They're the engine of the team.

332
00:18:24,080 --> 00:18:24,420
Right.

333
00:18:24,580 --> 00:18:28,230
You hear things like she is a real workhorse in the office, at home.

334
00:18:28,470 --> 00:18:28,890
Doesn't matter.

335
00:18:28,910 --> 00:18:30,530
She always delivers quality work on time.

336
00:18:30,800 --> 00:18:31,240
Exactly.

337
00:18:31,280 --> 00:18:32,740
Or maybe during promotion discussions.

338
00:18:33,160 --> 00:18:35,830
He really deserves this absolute workhorse for years.

339
00:18:36,250 --> 00:18:38,410
Always takes on extra tasks, beats his targets.

340
00:18:38,820 --> 00:18:40,720
It's like the ultimate compliment for dedication.

341
00:18:40,900 --> 00:18:45,510
It really emphasizes the value of that dedication, reliability, high performance.

342
00:18:46,030 --> 00:18:50,150
Being called a workhorse means you add value, you drive results, you can be counted on.

343
00:18:50,690 --> 00:18:54,540
It often leads directly to recognition, promotions, raises.

344
00:18:54,800 --> 00:18:55,200
Makes sense.

345
00:18:55,720 --> 00:19:04,150
Aspiring to be seen as a workhorse for improving business English and your career means embodying those valued traits.

346
00:19:04,930 --> 00:19:08,190
Diligence, commitment, consistent productivity.

347
00:19:08,590 --> 00:19:10,670
It's the ideal professional standard, really.

348
00:19:10,650 --> 00:19:18,240
Okay, when things get really urgent, maybe a crisis or a massive deadline looms, phrase 13 comes out, all hands on deck.

349
00:19:18,500 --> 00:19:18,660
Yeah.

350
00:19:18,900 --> 00:19:19,720
Comes from sailing rank.

351
00:19:20,300 --> 00:19:21,440
Everyone needed immediately.

352
00:19:21,660 --> 00:19:27,710
Spot on, nautical origin, desperate times on a ship needed every sailor all hands on deck for something critical.

353
00:19:28,190 --> 00:19:34,740
In business, it means everyone on the team, maybe the whole department or company needs to jump in on an urgent, all-consuming project or situation.

354
00:19:34,880 --> 00:19:35,620
So serious.

355
00:19:35,640 --> 00:19:36,120
Very.

356
00:19:36,100 --> 00:19:42,150
It can also refer to an all hands meeting, which means everyone has to attend, usually for a major announcement.

357
00:19:42,470 --> 00:19:49,120
So facing a tight deadline, a manager might say, OK, people, we need all hands on deck if we're going to get this client proposal finished on time.

358
00:19:49,340 --> 00:19:49,500
Yeah.

359
00:19:50,060 --> 00:19:50,800
A call to action.

360
00:19:50,940 --> 00:19:53,260
And that all hands meeting sounds pretty important, too.

361
00:19:53,780 --> 00:19:56,570
CEO just called an all hands meeting for Thursday morning.

362
00:19:57,250 --> 00:19:58,510
Mandatory attendance.

363
00:19:58,750 --> 00:20:01,860
It signals a critical situation needing collective effort.

364
00:20:02,150 --> 00:20:12,420
Urgency, teamwork, shared responsibility, understanding all hands on deck when improving business English helps you recognize top priorities and signals when maximum effort is required.

365
00:20:12,980 --> 00:20:13,990
It's a rally cry.

366
00:20:14,460 --> 00:20:18,960
And speaking of management styles, phrase 14 often gets a sigh, maybe an eye roll.

367
00:20:19,540 --> 00:20:20,560
To micromanage.

368
00:20:21,480 --> 00:20:22,790
Universally disliked, I think.

369
00:20:22,970 --> 00:20:23,990
Oh, absolutely dreaded.

370
00:20:24,390 --> 00:20:29,630
To micromanage is when a manager tries to control every tiny detail of their employees' work.

371
00:20:29,650 --> 00:20:30,630
Checking constantly.

372
00:20:31,110 --> 00:20:32,280
Telling you how to do everything.

373
00:20:32,300 --> 00:20:32,780
Exactly.

374
00:20:32,980 --> 00:20:35,140
Interfering with tasks that should be autonomous.

375
00:20:35,240 --> 00:20:40,680
It almost always leads to frustration, resentment, kills trust, and stifles any creativity or initiative.

376
00:20:40,780 --> 00:20:41,120
Totally.

377
00:20:41,340 --> 00:20:42,220
You hear people venting.

378
00:20:42,550 --> 00:20:47,170
My boss tries to micromanage everything down to how I word my emails or the employees pleading.

379
00:20:47,630 --> 00:20:49,160
Nobody likes being micromanaged.

380
00:20:49,220 --> 00:20:50,900
Can't you just trust me to do the job?

381
00:20:51,340 --> 00:20:52,840
It just crushes morale.

382
00:20:53,140 --> 00:20:56,510
And this raises big questions about leadership, about psychological safety.

383
00:20:57,010 --> 00:21:02,420
It highlights the huge negative impact on autonomy, morale and often productivity itself.

384
00:21:02,670 --> 00:21:03,810
Because people disengage.

385
00:21:03,950 --> 00:21:04,170
Right.

386
00:21:04,630 --> 00:21:08,680
When people feel trusted, empowered, they're usually more engaged, more innovative.

387
00:21:09,320 --> 00:21:13,830
Micromanagement does the opposite, can lead to high turnover, loss of good people.

388
00:21:14,570 --> 00:21:23,120
Understanding this phrase for improving business English helps you identify this difficult style and also talk about fostering a more empowering work environment.

389
00:21:23,580 --> 00:21:25,840
What actually helps teams perform well?

390
00:21:25,960 --> 00:21:35,560
Okay, let's shift again this time to phrases focused on business strategy and efficiency, the language of growth, alignment, and staying focused.

391
00:21:35,780 --> 00:21:38,180
First up, phrase 15, to align.

392
00:21:38,840 --> 00:21:41,950
Remember that silent G, it's not just about lining things up physically, right?

393
00:21:42,130 --> 00:21:43,290
What's the strategic meaning?

394
00:21:43,270 --> 00:21:43,610
Right.

395
00:21:43,810 --> 00:21:50,220
Silent G. Strategically to align means being in agreement, in harmony, or supporting something.

396
00:21:50,540 --> 00:21:55,070
Could be aligning with company goals, value strategies, or even aligning with other organizations.

397
00:21:55,210 --> 00:21:56,940
It basically means there's a good fit.

398
00:21:56,960 --> 00:21:58,200
So objectives match up.

399
00:21:58,300 --> 00:21:58,520
Yes.

400
00:21:58,740 --> 00:21:59,780
Efforts are coordinated.

401
00:21:59,820 --> 00:22:01,890
There's a clear, unified direction.

402
00:22:01,950 --> 00:22:09,590
OK, so a project might get turned down because this proposal doesn't really align with our core strategic goals this year.

403
00:22:09,610 --> 00:22:10,270
Exactly.

404
00:22:10,450 --> 00:22:11,550
Or maybe about partnerships.

405
00:22:12,050 --> 00:22:15,760
Our company aligns itself with organizations that share similar ethical standards.

406
00:22:16,340 --> 00:22:18,310
It's about everything pointing the same way.

407
00:22:18,290 --> 00:22:23,510
It's absolutely critical for strategic decisions, for allocating resources effectively.

408
00:22:24,290 --> 00:22:32,580
When you talk about alignment, you're making sure actions, projects, partnerships, even individual work all serve the company's main objectives.

409
00:22:32,600 --> 00:22:33,720
Avoids wasted effort.

410
00:22:33,740 --> 00:22:34,360
Precisely.

411
00:22:34,400 --> 00:22:36,060
Everything moves forward together.

412
00:22:36,680 --> 00:22:43,650
Using to align in your business English shows you think strategically, you see the bigger picture, how all the pieces fit the mission.

413
00:22:44,110 --> 00:22:47,310
Following closely is phrase 16, to streamline.

414
00:22:47,900 --> 00:22:51,790
Makes me think of making something smooth, efficient, like a fast train or flowing water.

415
00:22:52,250 --> 00:22:53,030
That's a great visual.

416
00:22:53,390 --> 00:23:00,540
To streamline means making a process, a system, an operation, maybe even the whole company structure much more effective and efficient.

417
00:23:00,860 --> 00:23:15,630
By finding and cutting out unnecessary steps, getting rid of redundancies, reducing scattered effort, optimizing workflow, the goal is always to lower costs, boost productivity, increase efficiency, making it smooth, logical, productive.

418
00:23:15,800 --> 00:23:16,100
Right.

419
00:23:16,260 --> 00:23:25,670
So someone focused on improvement might say, if we really take time to streamline this customer onboarding process, will lower costs ain't ain't beat customers happier?

420
00:23:25,750 --> 00:23:26,530
Perfect example.

421
00:23:26,850 --> 00:23:28,050
Or maybe a bigger move.

422
00:23:28,070 --> 00:23:33,600
The company decided to streamline its structure by cutting layers of management, aiming to be more agile.

423
00:23:33,620 --> 00:23:35,760
It's about making things leaner, faster.

424
00:23:35,740 --> 00:23:40,850
It emphasizes optimizing operations, boosting productivity, hitting goals by removing waste.

425
00:23:41,470 --> 00:23:49,000
A streamlined process means less wasted time, fewer wasted resources, often better quality, leads to better profits, competitive edge.

426
00:23:49,840 --> 00:23:57,330
Using to streamline for improving business English shows you understand operational excellence, continuous improvement, key for driving efficiency.

427
00:23:57,790 --> 00:24:01,880
For businesses aiming to grow, especially startups, phrase 17 scalable is huge.

428
00:24:02,160 --> 00:24:04,180
Almost a buzzword, but it means something very specific.

429
00:24:04,570 --> 00:24:05,950
It's definitely more than a buzzword.

430
00:24:06,250 --> 00:24:07,670
It's fundamental for growth.

431
00:24:08,630 --> 00:24:18,150
Scalable describes the ability of a business or a system or product to expand efficiently, to grow profits without costs or effort increasing at the same rate.

432
00:24:18,270 --> 00:24:20,110
So growth isn't strictly linear.

433
00:24:20,390 --> 00:24:20,870
Exactly.

434
00:24:20,910 --> 00:24:23,940
It signifies potential for exponential growth.

435
00:24:24,360 --> 00:24:30,950
Think about teaching English, a physical classroom, limited seats, one teacher, not very scalable.

436
00:24:31,130 --> 00:24:31,370
Right.

437
00:24:31,430 --> 00:24:37,480
But teaching English on YouTube, you could reach millions without the cost of buildings or teachers increasing proportionally.

438
00:24:37,900 --> 00:24:39,360
That's highly scalable.

439
00:24:39,340 --> 00:24:40,810
Ah, that example nails it.

440
00:24:40,950 --> 00:24:46,580
So you could say this software startup is small now, but the tech is highly scalable, huge growth potential.

441
00:24:46,960 --> 00:24:52,590
Or she convinced investors her idea was scalable, which led to big profits much faster than expected.

442
00:24:53,010 --> 00:24:57,120
It's about building something that can grow without proportionally growing pains.

443
00:24:57,140 --> 00:24:58,820
Enables that hockey stick curve.

444
00:24:59,040 --> 00:25:02,600
It's a critical concept today, especially in tech and VC worlds.

445
00:25:03,140 --> 00:25:09,570
Expanding reach without linearly increasing resources, people, infrastructure, costs, that's the magic.

446
00:25:10,350 --> 00:25:14,060
Scalability is often key to rapid, efficient, sustainable growth.

447
00:25:14,680 --> 00:25:21,990
Understanding scalable, for improving business English, shows you grasp modern business dynamics, signals a forward-thinking view.

448
00:25:22,090 --> 00:25:24,520
Now let's talk about staying focused.

449
00:25:24,540 --> 00:25:26,090
Big challenge in meetings sometimes.

450
00:25:26,610 --> 00:25:28,920
Phrase 18 or lost in the weeds.

451
00:25:29,640 --> 00:25:31,390
Makes me picture being stuck in tall grass.

452
00:25:31,670 --> 00:25:32,670
Can't see where you're going.

453
00:25:32,740 --> 00:25:33,400
Perfect image.

454
00:25:33,720 --> 00:25:37,610
Being in the weeds means getting too focused on tiny, unimportant details.

455
00:25:38,250 --> 00:25:40,310
Losing sight of the big picture, the main goal.

456
00:25:40,470 --> 00:25:42,230
Getting sidetracked by minutia.

457
00:25:42,250 --> 00:25:42,730
Exactly.

458
00:25:42,770 --> 00:25:44,520
Very common pitfall in discussions.

459
00:25:44,860 --> 00:25:47,280
Wastes time, doesn't move the main objective forward.

460
00:25:47,440 --> 00:25:53,410
So a meeting leader might gently say, OK, folks, let's try not to get lost in the weeds on this minor point.

461
00:25:53,430 --> 00:25:55,070
Let's keep our eyes on the main goal here.

462
00:25:55,270 --> 00:25:56,760
Yeah, a polite redirection.

463
00:25:57,300 --> 00:25:59,520
Or if the tech details get too specific.

464
00:26:00,120 --> 00:26:02,730
All right, everyone, let's pull back from the weeds for a moment.

465
00:26:03,110 --> 00:26:05,410
We can hash out those implementation details later.

466
00:26:05,950 --> 00:26:07,750
Let's refocus on the overall strategy.

467
00:26:08,270 --> 00:26:17,870
It's a really useful tool for facilitators, for managers, guides discussions back to what matters, keeps things productive, ensures time and energy are spent well.

468
00:26:18,770 --> 00:26:28,920
Using or understanding in the weeds for improving business English helps you contribute to more focused, efficient conversations, shows you value strategic time management.

469
00:26:28,980 --> 00:26:32,210
Closely related is phrase 19, to circle back.

470
00:26:32,850 --> 00:26:35,680
The visual of a circle makes sense returning to an earlier point.

471
00:26:36,040 --> 00:26:42,750
Yes, to circle back means returning to a topic, idea, or question mentioned earlier, or maybe reconsidering it later.

472
00:26:42,790 --> 00:26:43,930
When would you use it?

473
00:26:44,400 --> 00:26:49,610
Often when a discussion goes off on a tangent, but the point raised was actually important and needs addressing later.

474
00:26:49,630 --> 00:26:53,740
It's a polite way to temporarily pause that thread without losing it.

475
00:26:53,860 --> 00:26:54,120
Okay.

