This is extremely polite.

711
00:40:02,690 --> 00:40:11,580
It's a really considerate way to ask for someone's time or help, especially if you know or just assume they might be busy or focused on something else.

712
00:40:11,600 --> 00:40:11,800
Right.

713
00:40:11,860 --> 00:40:13,060
You don't want to just barge in.

714
00:40:13,100 --> 00:40:13,920
Exactly.

715
00:40:14,120 --> 00:40:16,640
It shows respect for their work, their schedule.

716
00:40:17,100 --> 00:40:23,250
Instead of just demanding their attention, you're giving them the courtesy to respond when they actually can pause and focus on your request.

717
00:40:23,730 --> 00:40:27,220
It's like workplace empathy in action.

718
00:40:27,480 --> 00:40:32,570
So instead of marching over and saying, look at this report now, you'd be much smoother.

719
00:40:32,670 --> 00:40:36,760
Maybe saying, hey, when you have a minute, could you glance at this report for me?

720
00:40:37,360 --> 00:40:39,820
Or maybe a bit more direct, but still polite.

721
00:40:40,270 --> 00:40:42,090
Do you have a minute to look at this report right now?

722
00:40:42,690 --> 00:40:46,120
It just makes the whole interaction feel less demanding, more collaborative.

723
00:40:46,100 --> 00:40:46,780
It really does.

724
00:40:46,820 --> 00:40:51,020
It elevates the interaction, respecting people's time, acknowledging their workload.

725
00:40:51,040 --> 00:40:51,500
That's vital.

726
00:40:51,800 --> 00:40:55,700
It builds positive working relationships based on mutual consideration.

727
00:40:56,070 --> 00:41:03,730
It's a small language thing, almost invisible, but it has a big impact on how people see you and honestly, how easily you can get things done together.

728
00:41:04,530 --> 00:41:10,000
Mastering this kind of politeness is a big step in improving your business English beyond just words.

729
00:41:10,360 --> 00:41:11,500
It's cultural fluency.

730
00:41:11,720 --> 00:41:14,700
OK, this next one paints a cool picture, I think.

731
00:41:14,900 --> 00:41:18,090
to bounce some ideas off of you or sometimes just off you.

732
00:41:18,510 --> 00:41:21,300
What's this bouncing thing really mean at work?

733
00:41:21,320 --> 00:41:24,150
It sounds playful, but it feels professional.

734
00:41:24,300 --> 00:41:26,420
It's pretty much like the image suggests.

735
00:41:26,440 --> 00:41:33,350
You're sort of metaphorically tossing an idea at someone to see how it, well, bounces back, to get their gut reaction, their opinion.

736
00:41:33,750 --> 00:41:34,070
Oh, okay.

737
00:41:34,090 --> 00:41:38,380
But the key thing is you're not expecting perfect answers or final solutions.

738
00:41:38,700 --> 00:41:39,180
Not at all.

739
00:41:39,400 --> 00:41:42,540
The main goal is just to refine it together, get some informal feedback.

740
00:41:42,560 --> 00:41:44,880
Think of it like a mini two-person brainstorm.

741
00:41:45,040 --> 00:41:46,320
So testing the waters a bit.

742
00:41:46,340 --> 00:41:46,920
Exactly.

743
00:41:46,940 --> 00:41:52,350
You're putting a raw idea out there, seeing if it resonates, getting some early thoughts to make it better.

744
00:41:52,330 --> 00:41:57,500
What's great about bouncing ideas is that it makes it OK to share something that's not perfect yet.

745
00:41:57,720 --> 00:41:57,960
Right.

746
00:41:58,020 --> 00:41:58,540
Less pressure.

747
00:41:58,880 --> 00:41:59,180
Yeah.

748
00:41:59,240 --> 00:42:03,470
It encourages more creative thinking early on because the stakes are low.

749
00:42:03,850 --> 00:42:06,030
It actually speeds up innovation in a way.

750
00:42:06,130 --> 00:42:10,840
So it's not like asking for the finished products, just getting a feel for it.

751
00:42:10,860 --> 00:42:13,920
Like you might grab coffee with someone and say, hey, want to grab coffee?

752
00:42:14,080 --> 00:42:16,280
I'd love to bounce some ideas off you for this assignment.

753
00:42:16,300 --> 00:42:16,470
Perfect.

754
00:42:16,450 --> 00:42:17,290
Perfect example.

755
00:42:17,310 --> 00:42:22,610
Or if you're stuck on something, I've been mulling over this project and I'd really like to bounce some ideas off.

756
00:42:22,630 --> 00:42:23,490
You got a few minutes.

757
00:42:23,950 --> 00:42:26,820
It shows you value their input even early on.

758
00:42:27,140 --> 00:42:30,800
It really connects to brainstorming and creative problem solving.

759
00:42:31,420 --> 00:42:34,440
It fosters that open discussion, that iterative process.

760
00:42:34,780 --> 00:42:40,210
You're inviting quick, informal feedback that helps shape the idea before it's all formal and polished.

761
00:42:40,230 --> 00:42:40,790
Makes sense.

762
00:42:40,810 --> 00:42:46,430
It's a proactive, collaborative way to work with people, tapping into that collective intelligence quickly.

763
00:42:46,410 --> 00:42:50,600
shows you're open-minded, which is a big part of professional communication.

764
00:42:50,820 --> 00:42:58,910
Okay, speaking of tapping into intelligence, our next one, to pick your brains, always sounded a bit weird to me at first.

765
00:42:59,210 --> 00:43:00,350
A little graphic, maybe?

766
00:43:00,570 --> 00:43:02,670
Aha, yeah, it does sound a bit odd, taken literally.

767
00:43:03,030 --> 00:43:10,260
But here's where it gets really interesting, because it's so common and useful, even if the image is strange.

768
00:43:10,340 --> 00:43:10,660
Right.

769
00:43:10,920 --> 00:43:13,630
Its meaning is actually very straightforward and respected.

770
00:43:14,070 --> 00:43:22,080
It just means you're respectfully asking for input, advice or details from someone who has more experience or knowledge on a topic than you do.

771
00:43:22,400 --> 00:43:23,900
So it's about expertise.

772
00:43:23,920 --> 00:43:24,420
Exactly.

773
00:43:24,520 --> 00:43:29,530
It's a deferential way to tap into their expertise, acknowledging their insights.

774
00:43:30,050 --> 00:43:31,310
You're not demanding anything.

775
00:43:31,370 --> 00:43:36,550
You're humbly asking for their wisdom, almost like asking for a mini mentoring session.

776
00:43:36,530 --> 00:43:45,270
Okay, so putting that in context, you might say to a longtime colleague, I know you've been here five years, so I'd love to pick your brains about what it's really like to work here.

777
00:43:45,490 --> 00:43:46,750
Mm-hmm, perfect.

778
00:43:46,770 --> 00:43:49,970
Or maybe more strategically, could we hop on a quick call this week?

779
00:43:50,350 --> 00:43:53,920
I'd really appreciate a chance to pick your brains about your outlook for next quarter.

780
00:43:54,440 --> 00:43:57,020
It's all about learning from people who know more, basically.

781
00:43:57,070 --> 00:44:02,390
And this phrase really underlines the value of mentorship, of knowledge sharing in a company.

782
00:44:03,040 --> 00:44:07,940
It frames the request as a professional courtesy, a genuine wish to learn, not a demand.

783
00:44:08,200 --> 00:44:15,910
Practically speaking, it's a super efficient way to get insights, avoid mistakes, and speed up your own learning without reinventing the wheel.

784
00:44:17,030 --> 00:44:22,550
Using it shows you're proactive about learning and respect expertise, both great professional traits.

785
00:44:22,840 --> 00:44:26,430
Okay, let's shift gears slightly to efficient information exchange.

786
00:44:27,290 --> 00:44:29,330
Speed and clarity are huge these days.

787
00:44:29,800 --> 00:44:33,220
Our next phrase, number four from the outline, is first thing in the morning.

788
00:44:34,290 --> 00:44:38,540
I notice people sometimes try to say the first thing, but it's usually just first thing, right?

789
00:44:38,840 --> 00:44:39,480
More direct.

790
00:44:39,510 --> 00:44:40,170
Absolutely.

791
00:44:40,650 --> 00:44:42,560
It's a fixed phrase, almost an idiom.

792
00:44:43,100 --> 00:44:51,690
It means doing something right away or immediately when the workday starts, usually without the because it means the start of the business day, not necessarily, you know, sunrise.

793
00:44:52,570 --> 00:44:55,420
It sets a really clear expectation for prompt action.

794
00:44:55,980 --> 00:44:57,680
But there's a catch sometimes.

795
00:44:57,720 --> 00:45:02,330
If you're working with people in totally different time zones, first thing in the morning can get confusing.

796
00:45:02,590 --> 00:45:03,290
Who's morning?

797
00:45:03,270 --> 00:45:04,270
Ah, good point.

798
00:45:04,430 --> 00:45:04,910
Very true.

799
00:45:05,510 --> 00:45:13,250
So in those cases, it's much better to be specific, like by 9 a.m. Eastern or before the start of your day, just to be crystal clear.

800
00:45:13,710 --> 00:45:13,870
Right.

801
00:45:14,030 --> 00:45:15,010
Clarity is key there.

802
00:45:15,340 --> 00:45:18,720
So personally, someone might say first thing in the morning, I make coffee.

803
00:45:19,700 --> 00:45:20,360
That's a routine.

804
00:45:21,070 --> 00:45:25,510
But at work, if you're waiting on something, if I don't hear back today, I'll call her first thing in the morning.

805
00:45:27,170 --> 00:45:28,110
to clear commitment.

806
00:45:28,210 --> 00:45:28,870
Exactly.

807
00:45:28,970 --> 00:45:32,410
Or wrapping up, sorry, can't finish the report today, but I will do it first thing in the morning.

808
00:45:32,850 --> 00:45:33,800
No ambiguity.

809
00:45:34,500 --> 00:45:38,320
And its value in setting those expectations and showing commitment is huge.

810
00:45:38,780 --> 00:45:43,370
In business, especially with projects or clients, knowing when things will get done is crucial.

811
00:45:44,390 --> 00:45:51,740
First thing gives that clear promise without needing an exact time, which can be efficient internally as long as everyone's roughly on the same schedule.

812
00:45:51,840 --> 00:45:53,540
Okay, sticking with efficiency.

813
00:45:53,880 --> 00:45:56,690
Next up is to hop on a call.

814
00:45:56,710 --> 00:45:59,790
This one feels like it's exploded, especially with remote work.

815
00:46:00,090 --> 00:46:03,700
It sounds casual, like literally hopping, but it's totally professional.

816
00:46:03,850 --> 00:46:05,970
It really captures the modern vibe, doesn't it?

817
00:46:06,390 --> 00:46:16,850
To hop on a call means starting a conversation, often pretty spontaneously, maybe with short notice over the phone, or more likely now, Zoom, Teams, something like that.

818
00:46:17,110 --> 00:46:25,760
It implies a quick, direct chat about something that's probably easier or faster to sort out by talking in real time, that back and forth, instead of writing long emails.

819
00:46:26,260 --> 00:46:28,760
It suggests agility, immediacy.

820
00:46:29,160 --> 00:46:34,930
So if a client has urgent questions, you might ping a colleague, hey, got a minute to hop on a call.

821
00:46:35,230 --> 00:46:38,360
Client has some questions that might be easier to discuss.

822
00:46:38,720 --> 00:46:44,830
Or if an email thread is getting crazy complicated, I was going to email again, but maybe it's easier if we just talk.

823
00:46:44,850 --> 00:46:46,050
Can we hop on a call real quick?

824
00:46:46,390 --> 00:46:47,190
Gets through the clutter.

825
00:46:47,320 --> 00:46:55,450
It really signals a preference for talking now instead of emailing back and forth, especially for urgent things, complex issues, maybe sensitive stuff.

826
00:46:55,470 --> 00:46:58,300
It shows you want quick resolution or collaboration.

827
00:46:58,440 --> 00:46:59,040
Makes sense.

828
00:46:59,060 --> 00:47:07,390
For people working on their business English, knowing when to suggest helping on a call versus sending another email is a key strategic communication skill.

829
00:47:07,410 --> 00:47:10,370
It affects how quickly things get done and even relationships.

830
00:47:10,850 --> 00:47:13,080
It's about picking the right channel for the job.

831
00:47:13,060 --> 00:47:20,260
And speaking of quick communication, the next one, or rather pair of phrases, uses verbs that really scream speed.

832
00:47:21,630 --> 00:47:24,930
To shoot off an email or to fire off an email?

833
00:47:25,840 --> 00:47:28,540
Shoot and fire sound fast, decisive.

834
00:47:28,720 --> 00:47:29,600
They absolutely do.

835
00:47:29,660 --> 00:47:33,130
Both mean sending an email, maybe even a quick text, very quickly.

836
00:47:33,170 --> 00:47:37,880
The emphasis is on speed, efficiency, often being concise.

837
00:47:38,260 --> 00:47:38,520
Right.

838
00:47:38,700 --> 00:47:47,230
It implies you're sending it without much fuss, not overthinking it, just getting the essential info out there fast, being agile with your writing.

839
00:47:47,210 --> 00:47:53,140
So maybe before a big meeting, I'm just going to fire off an email with these key questions before we meet the client so they're prepped.

840
00:47:53,620 --> 00:47:56,860
Or if you need info from someone, could you just shoot me an email with those numbers?

841
00:47:56,920 --> 00:47:58,180
I'll tackle it first thing tomorrow.

842
00:47:58,460 --> 00:47:59,780
See, combining them already.

843
00:47:59,900 --> 00:48:00,400
Exactly.

844
00:48:00,600 --> 00:48:06,150
It shows its usefulness when you need quick, decisive communication, maybe getting info out fast or needing a quick response.

845
00:48:06,690 --> 00:48:07,190
And you're right.

846
00:48:07,250 --> 00:48:13,360
Seeing how they link up, like with first thing, shows how these phrases build on each other for efficient, natural business talk.

847
00:48:13,380 --> 00:48:14,960
Direct, but still professional.

848
00:48:14,940 --> 00:48:16,920
Okay, last one in this section, number eight.

849
00:48:17,140 --> 00:48:18,150
This one has a great visual.

850
00:48:18,410 --> 00:48:20,420
To keep someone in the loop.

851
00:48:20,440 --> 00:48:25,130
The loop makes you think of connection, inclusion, information flow.

852
00:48:25,160 --> 00:48:25,400
Right.

853
00:48:25,620 --> 00:48:26,180
Precisely.

854
00:48:27,010 --> 00:48:38,840
To keep someone in the loop means making sure a specific person or maybe a whole team is consistently included, updated, and gets all the necessary info about a project, a discussion, whatever's going on.

855
00:48:39,120 --> 00:48:40,080
So they're not left out.

856
00:48:40,360 --> 00:48:40,680
Exactly.

857
00:48:40,700 --> 00:48:42,600
They're not left out of the communication flow.

858
00:48:42,620 --> 00:48:43,570
They know what's happening.

859
00:48:44,050 --> 00:48:45,590
But there's a nuance here.

860
00:48:45,910 --> 00:48:46,010
Okay.

861
00:48:46,450 --> 00:48:48,530
You need to know what's necessary information.

862
00:48:48,590 --> 00:48:49,870
You don't want to overloop people.

863
00:48:49,850 --> 00:48:52,640
CCing everyone on everything.

864
00:48:52,660 --> 00:48:52,880
Right.

865
00:48:53,160 --> 00:48:57,400
Bombarding colleagues with irrelevant stuff can cause information overload.

866
00:48:57,960 --> 00:49:00,120
Then they might miss the important updates.

867
00:49:00,140 --> 00:49:00,810
It's a balance.

868
00:49:01,050 --> 00:49:02,730
That's a really good point about over looping.

869
00:49:03,350 --> 00:49:07,430
So if you missed a meeting, you might ask, hey, sorry, I missed the meeting yesterday.

870
00:49:07,760 --> 00:49:10,140
Could you keep me in the loop and forward any notes or emails?

871
00:49:10,360 --> 00:49:10,700
Yep.

872
00:49:10,720 --> 00:49:11,240
Perfect use.

873
00:49:11,460 --> 00:49:17,330
Or if the boss needs to know everything, the boss wants us to keep her in the loop on all major progress for this project.

874
00:49:17,490 --> 00:49:23,840
This phrase is super important for good teamwork, transparency, and just making sure information flows properly.

875
00:49:23,860 --> 00:49:30,350
It helps avoid miscommunication, keeps everyone aligned, and stops people from feeling isolated or out of touch.

876
00:49:30,430 --> 00:49:32,690
Yeah, feeling left out is demotivating.

877
00:49:32,870 --> 00:49:33,250
Totally.

878
00:49:33,570 --> 00:49:39,100
It's about keeping the team cohesive and informed, which helps projects succeed and keeps morale up.

879
00:49:39,580 --> 00:49:44,460
For improving business English, using this well shows great team communication skills.

880
00:49:44,440 --> 00:49:45,400
All right, let's pivot now.

881
00:49:45,600 --> 00:49:47,220
We've covered those everyday interactions.

882
00:49:47,750 --> 00:49:55,050
Now let's move into strategic thinking and project management, where innovation and getting things done right are really key.

883
00:49:55,530 --> 00:49:56,680
The execution side.

884
00:49:57,000 --> 00:50:03,280
Our first phrase here, number nine, might seem basic, but it's fundamental to creative work, to brainstorm.

885
00:50:03,530 --> 00:50:04,610
You can almost picture it, right?

886
00:50:04,890 --> 00:50:08,070
A busy meeting room, whiteboards full, ideas flying everywhere.

887
00:50:08,050 --> 00:50:08,970
You definitely can.

888
00:50:09,050 --> 00:50:13,620
And while brainstorm just means generating ideas, in business, it's usually a bit more structured.

889
00:50:13,680 --> 00:50:19,180
It's about generating lots of ideas, often in a group, where the main rule is to get all ideas out there.

890
00:50:19,360 --> 00:50:20,820
No bad ideas initially.

891
00:50:20,840 --> 00:50:21,320
Exactly.

892
00:50:21,690 --> 00:50:24,250
Regardless of how good, bad, or crazy they seem at first.

893
00:50:24,790 --> 00:50:31,500
The idea is to get a free flow of thoughts, encourage wild thinking, to find out what really works or what might lead to a breakthrough.

894
00:50:32,040 --> 00:50:34,340
Quantity first, then you evaluate the quality.

895
00:50:34,720 --> 00:50:35,500
Okay.

896
00:50:35,520 --> 00:50:36,540
Quantity, then quality.

897
00:50:36,560 --> 00:50:37,260
That makes sense.

898
00:50:37,240 --> 00:50:40,850
That initial unfiltered burst is vital for real innovation.

899
00:50:40,910 --> 00:50:44,460
So you might lay out a plan like tomorrow in our strategy meeting.

900
00:50:44,880 --> 00:50:48,820
The first hour is purely to brainstorm new marketing ideas for Q3.

901
00:50:49,280 --> 00:50:50,010
No holding back?

902
00:50:50,210 --> 00:50:50,790
Yep.

903
00:50:50,810 --> 00:50:57,080
Or if there's a problem, our team got together last week to brainstorm better ways to help our customers with that recurring issue.

904
00:50:57,540 --> 00:51:00,700
It's about that initial creative explosion before you start filtering.

905
00:51:00,840 --> 00:51:06,320
It really connects to fostering creativity, encouraging different ways of thinking, solving problems together.

906
00:51:06,360 --> 00:51:10,120
It's crucial in the early stages of any project or new idea.

907
00:51:10,160 --> 00:51:13,170
It's where different perspectives mix to spark something new.

908
00:51:13,330 --> 00:51:19,050
And being good at facilitating or participating in brainstorming is a key business communication skill.

909
00:51:19,070 --> 00:51:24,460
Okay, so once a project is done or maybe even just hit a major milestone, our next phrase, number 10, comes in.

910
00:51:25,080 --> 00:51:26,660
To debrief.

911
00:51:26,680 --> 00:51:29,000
It sounds kind of serious, like after a mission or something.

912
00:51:29,200 --> 00:51:30,470
That's a great analogy.

913
00:51:30,490 --> 00:51:31,730
It fits perfectly.

914
00:51:31,770 --> 00:51:39,680
To debrief means to systematically summarize, review, or share info after something happens.

915
00:51:40,080 --> 00:51:42,730
A project, a campaign, a critical event.

916
00:51:42,930 --> 00:51:43,750
Like a post-mortem.

917
00:51:44,030 --> 00:51:47,000
Sort of, but often focus more on learning for the future.

918
00:51:47,140 --> 00:51:55,590
It's usually a meeting to talk about what went well, what didn't, maybe what was unexpected, and crucially, how to learn from it all to do better next time.

919
00:51:56,000 --> 00:51:56,180
Okay.

920
00:51:56,200 --> 00:51:56,860
And it's flexible.

921
00:51:56,900 --> 00:52:02,670
You can use it as a verb, we need to debrief the team, or a noun, let's schedule a debrief.

922
00:52:03,070 --> 00:52:10,570
So after a big project wraps up, you could say, okay, once this project is fully delivered, let's set up a meeting to debrief and capture lessons learned.

923
00:52:11,270 --> 00:52:17,990
Or if it already happened, we had a debrief this morning and decided to change a few things in the process for the next time we do this kind of project.

924
00:52:18,010 --> 00:52:19,590
It's all about learning from experience.

925
00:52:19,740 --> 00:52:26,270
And if you think bigger picture, debriefing is absolutely essential for organizations to grow and adapt.

926
00:52:26,820 --> 00:52:31,780
It builds a culture of learning, drives process improvement, helps people develop professionally.

927
00:52:31,820 --> 00:52:35,950
It makes sure successes get repeated and failures become valuable lessons.

928
00:52:36,590 --> 00:52:37,710
It's not just a meeting.

929
00:52:37,730 --> 00:52:41,160
It's a strategic feedback loop for getting better.

930
00:52:41,240 --> 00:52:45,930
Okay, now for a couple of phrases that really get to the heart of work ethic.

931
00:52:45,990 --> 00:52:46,730
Yeah.

932
00:52:46,750 --> 00:52:52,460
The next one, number 11, is unfortunately pretty common and has a very negative feel.

933
00:52:52,920 --> 00:52:53,480
A slacker.

934
00:52:53,960 --> 00:52:55,100
Nobody wants to be called that.

935
00:52:55,240 --> 00:52:56,510
No, definitely not.

936
00:52:56,530 --> 00:53:00,610
And while we all know what slacker means generally at work, it's more than just being lazy.

937
00:53:00,730 --> 00:53:07,380
It's someone who consistently doesn't do their fair share, avoids work, and often expects others to pick up their, well, slack.

938
00:53:07,580 --> 00:53:09,410
Yeah, makes things harder for everyone else.

939
00:53:09,430 --> 00:53:10,410
Hugely frustrating.

940
00:53:10,450 --> 00:53:11,470
They're demoralizing.

941
00:53:11,850 --> 00:53:12,590
Bad for the team.

942
00:53:12,690 --> 00:53:14,310
It's not just about their poor performance.

943
00:53:14,390 --> 00:53:16,050
It's the negative ripple effect.

944
00:53:16,090 --> 00:53:17,290
You hear the frustration, right?

945
00:53:17,310 --> 00:53:19,570
People might say, oh, he's such a slacker.

946
00:53:20,140 --> 00:53:24,420
Always late with his stuff, misses deadlines, and expects me to work overtime to cover for him.

947
00:53:24,800 --> 00:53:29,990
Or just generally, seems like every group project has one slacker who thinks everyone else will do the work.

948
00:53:30,690 --> 00:53:32,630
It really undermines teamwork.

949
00:53:32,610 --> 00:53:33,890
It absolutely does.

950
00:53:33,950 --> 00:53:38,520
This phrase captures that frustration, the resentment, the damage to morale.

951
00:53:39,020 --> 00:53:44,060
It's the total opposite of the positive work ethic, the reliability, the commitment everyone values.

952
00:53:44,640 --> 00:53:44,760
Right.

953
00:53:44,780 --> 00:53:47,310
Understanding this term isn't just vocabulary.

954
00:53:47,450 --> 00:53:52,070
It's recognizing that unspoken agreement in teamwork, everyone pulls their weight.

955
00:53:52,840 --> 00:53:57,940
Being able to describe this dynamic carefully, of course, is important for managing teams or giving feedback.

956
00:53:58,390 --> 00:54:03,360
So if slacker is the negative pole, our next phrase, number 12, is the positive opposite.

957
00:54:04,100 --> 00:54:04,780
A workhorse.

958
00:54:05,540 --> 00:54:07,160
This is the kind of person you want on your team.

959
00:54:07,260 --> 00:54:07,700
Exactly.

960
00:54:07,820 --> 00:54:12,170
A workhorse is someone known for exceptional dedication, diligence, just plain hard work.

961
00:54:12,550 --> 00:54:17,590
They're the ones who reliably get projects done, often going the extra mile without needing constant pushing.

962
00:54:17,750 --> 00:54:18,310
Dependable.

963
00:54:18,530 --> 00:54:19,290
Very dependable.

964
00:54:19,610 --> 00:54:20,710
Pillars of reliability.

965
00:54:21,090 --> 00:54:22,180
Committed to getting results.

966
00:54:22,450 --> 00:54:25,500
They usually drive a lot of value for the team and the company.

967
00:54:25,860 --> 00:54:26,760
Their output is high.

968
00:54:26,820 --> 00:54:28,080
Their commitment is strong.

969
00:54:28,060 --> 00:54:29,800
You often hear people praise like this.

970
00:54:30,240 --> 00:54:34,490
He is a real workhorse in the office, at home, doesn't matter.

971
00:54:34,550 --> 00:54:36,490
He always gets the job done and done well.

972
00:54:37,270 --> 00:54:40,440
Or talking about promotions, she really deserves that promotion.

973
00:54:40,500 --> 00:54:44,280
She's been a total workhorse on every project, delivering consistently.

974
00:54:44,300 --> 00:54:46,730
It's basically the ultimate compliment for work ethic.

975
00:54:46,710 --> 00:54:51,540
It really emphasizes the huge value of that dedication, reliability, and high performance.

976
00:54:52,080 --> 00:55:01,200
And it often directly links to real recognition promotions, more responsibility, because these are the people who add clear value, get results, and you can trust them to deliver.

977
00:55:01,380 --> 00:55:03,690
It's a highly valued professional trait.

978
00:55:03,850 --> 00:55:11,280
OK, when things get intense, deadlines are tight, or there's a big challenge, our next phrase, number 13, comes up.

979
00:55:11,300 --> 00:55:12,160
All hands on deck.

980
00:55:12,500 --> 00:55:13,640
I know it comes from ships, right?

981
00:55:13,680 --> 00:55:14,700
Everyone needed on deck.

982
00:55:14,930 --> 00:55:15,350
Spot on.

983
00:55:15,410 --> 00:55:16,230
That's the origin.

984
00:55:16,530 --> 00:55:19,650
A crisis on a ship, every sailor needed on deck immediately.

985
00:55:19,690 --> 00:55:22,170
In business, it's very similar figuratively.

986
00:55:22,210 --> 00:55:28,340
It means everyone, or at least everyone relevant, needs to jump in now on an urgent project or crisis.

987
00:55:28,470 --> 00:55:29,450
Urgency is key.

988
00:55:29,700 --> 00:55:30,900
Paramount.

989
00:55:30,920 --> 00:55:33,660
And interestingly, it has a second related meaning.

990
00:55:34,710 --> 00:55:35,950
An all-hands meeting.

991
00:55:35,990 --> 00:55:41,140
That's a mandatory meeting for everyone in the company or department, usually for big news or strategy updates.

992
00:55:41,620 --> 00:55:42,220
Oh, okay.

993
00:55:42,240 --> 00:55:42,900
Two meanings.

994
00:55:42,920 --> 00:55:43,020
Yeah.

995
00:55:43,180 --> 00:55:47,070
But the common thread is collective participation, immediate focus.

996
00:55:47,710 --> 00:55:55,640
So facing a tight deadline, a leader might say, OK, team, it's all hands on deck if we're going to finish this project on time for the client.

997
00:55:55,660 --> 00:55:55,920
Right.

998
00:55:56,110 --> 00:55:56,570
High stakes.

999
00:55:56,750 --> 00:55:58,630
And that all hands meeting sounds important.

1000
00:55:58,890 --> 00:56:02,050
The CEO just called an all hands meeting for Thursday afternoon.

1001
00:56:02,110 --> 00:56:03,140
Wonder what's up.

1002
00:56:03,620 --> 00:56:05,270
It definitely signals importance.

1003
00:56:05,570 --> 00:56:10,340
It's used in critical, high-pressure times needing coordinated effort and immediate attention.

1004
00:56:10,680 --> 00:56:14,600
It conveys urgency and teamwork, whether for a crisis or a major announcement.

1005
00:56:15,080 --> 00:56:18,250
Understanding the weight of all hands on deck helps you react appropriately.

1006
00:56:18,710 --> 00:56:23,960
And speaking of management, our next phrase, number 14, often gets a groan from employees.

1007
00:56:24,160 --> 00:56:25,980
To micromanage.

1008
00:56:26,000 --> 00:56:27,180
Sounds frustrating just saying it.

1009
00:56:27,260 --> 00:56:27,880
Oh, it really is.

1010
00:56:28,280 --> 00:56:33,670
To micromanage is a management style where the manager tries to control every tiny detail of their employee's work.

1011
00:56:33,830 --> 00:56:34,010
Yeah.

1012
00:56:34,310 --> 00:56:35,350
Way too much control.

1013
00:56:35,610 --> 00:56:36,930
Like looking over your shoulder constantly.

1014
00:56:36,950 --> 00:56:37,550
Exactly.

1015
00:56:37,700 --> 00:56:40,900
It stifles autonomy, kills initiative, suffocates creativity.

1016
00:56:41,400 --> 00:56:46,690
It usually leads to huge frustration, resentment, a breakdown of trust, and plummeting morale.

1017
00:56:47,510 --> 00:56:48,550
Why do managers do it?

1018
00:56:48,710 --> 00:56:51,760
Often it comes from their own anxiety or need for control.

1019
00:56:51,800 --> 00:56:55,020
But ironically, it creates more problems than it solves.

1020
00:56:55,040 --> 00:56:58,880
It really raises that question, you know, when does guiding become controlling?

1021
00:56:59,100 --> 00:57:00,220
Yeah, it's a fine line.

1022
00:57:00,260 --> 00:57:01,790
You hear employees complaining.

1023
00:57:01,810 --> 00:57:09,720
He tries to micromanage the team by telling everyone exactly how to do their job step by step, even stuff we've done dozens of times.

1024
00:57:10,510 --> 00:57:13,980
Or for the employee side, nobody likes being micromanaged.

1025
00:57:14,000 --> 00:57:15,940
Can't you just trust me to do my work?

1026
00:57:15,960 --> 00:57:16,540
Yeah.

1027
00:57:16,560 --> 00:57:17,840
It kills motivation.

1028
00:57:18,210 --> 00:57:25,160
This phrase highlights that huge negative impact on autonomy, trust and ultimately productivity and innovation.

1029
00:57:25,500 --> 00:57:28,980
When people feel trusted and empowered, they're usually more engaged and productive.

1030
00:57:29,240 --> 00:57:29,680
Makes sense.

1031
00:57:30,060 --> 00:57:38,310
Micromanagement leads to disengagement, feeling undervalued, often higher turnover and less innovation because people become afraid to try things.

1032
00:57:38,870 --> 00:57:41,210
It's a really costly leadership mistake.

1033
00:57:41,730 --> 00:57:47,240
Understanding that is key for improving communication, especially if you're in or moving into leadership.

1034
00:57:47,610 --> 00:57:49,270
Okay, let's shift focus again.

1035
00:57:50,030 --> 00:57:53,460
Now we're talking about phrases crucial for business strategy and efficiency.

1036
00:57:54,100 --> 00:57:58,240
Things that drive growth, enable innovation, and keep everyone focused.

1037
00:57:58,880 --> 00:58:01,510
First up, number 15, to align.

1038
00:58:02,110 --> 00:58:05,250
And remember that silent G. Right, silent G. Good reminder.

1039
00:58:05,490 --> 00:58:07,160
Beyond just lining things up physically.

1040
00:58:07,260 --> 00:58:07,640
Yeah.

1041
00:58:07,660 --> 00:58:10,060
What does align mean in a strategic business sense?

1042
00:58:10,160 --> 00:58:10,980
It feels deeper.

1043
00:58:10,960 --> 00:58:11,840
It is much deeper.

1044
00:58:12,000 --> 00:58:16,670
To align in business means being in agreement, in harmony, supporting the same direction.

1045
00:58:17,110 --> 00:58:21,160
It applies to goals, values, strategies, even partnerships with other companies.

1046
00:58:21,380 --> 00:58:26,950
It means there's a good fit, synergy, objectives are shared, efforts support each other, resources go towards a common goal.

1047
00:58:27,350 --> 00:58:29,930
When things align, there's less friction, more momentum.

1048
00:58:30,070 --> 00:58:38,220
So a decision might be explained like, that project doesn't align with our long-term goals, so we're not pursuing it, even though it looks good short-term.

1049
00:58:38,240 --> 00:58:38,740
Exactly.

1050
00:58:38,780 --> 00:58:39,840
Strategic fit.

1051
00:58:39,860 --> 00:58:40,820
Or about partnerships.

1052
00:58:41,460 --> 00:58:45,670
Our company aligns itself with organizations that share our values and have complementary goals.

1053
00:58:46,350 --> 00:58:49,270
It's all about making sure everything pulls in the same direction.

1054
00:58:49,350 --> 00:58:52,020
It's absolutely critical for strategic decisions.

1055
00:58:52,400 --> 00:58:57,740
It ensures actions, projects, partnerships all serve the company's core objectives and vision.

1056
00:58:58,430 --> 00:59:07,960
When everything's aligned, resources are used effectively, efforts are coordinated, and the whole organization moves forward together, not wasting energy on things pulling different ways.

1057
00:59:08,540 --> 00:59:09,960
Like a rowing team in sync.

1058
00:59:10,090 --> 00:59:14,140
Okay, following a line, we have phrase 16, to streamline.

1059
00:59:15,220 --> 00:59:21,000
When I hear that, I think of something sleek, efficient, like a smooth flow, optimizing things.

1060
00:59:21,140 --> 00:59:22,110
That's a perfect image.

1061
00:59:22,510 --> 00:59:30,140
To streamline means making a process, a system, even a company structure more effective and efficient, often more economical too.

1062
00:59:30,720 --> 00:59:45,250
By finding and reducing scattered efforts, cutting out unnecessary steps or complexities, optimizing the sequence of things, the goal is to lower costs, reduce waste, and increase efficiency, creating a smooth, logical, productive workflow.

1063
00:59:45,580 --> 00:59:54,430
So a manager might say, if we invest time now to streamline this complicated onboarding process, we'll lower costs and get new hires productive faster.

1064
00:59:55,310 --> 00:59:55,790
Makes sense.

1065
00:59:55,930 --> 01:00:02,800
Or maybe higher up, leadership streamline the company by reducing management layers, aiming for faster decisions and more agility.

1066
01:00:03,550 --> 01:00:07,430
It's about cutting the fat, removing bottlenecks, making things leaner and faster.

1067
01:00:07,710 --> 01:00:14,440
It really emphasizes its role in optimizing operations, improving productivity, achieving goals by cutting out the unnecessary.

1068
01:00:14,790 --> 01:00:19,360
A streamlined process means less wasted time, fewer errors, better use of resources.

1069
01:00:19,740 --> 01:00:23,390
That leads directly to better profits, higher output, and being more competitive.

1070
01:00:23,690 --> 01:00:26,140
It's a cornerstone of operational excellence.

1071
01:00:26,160 --> 01:00:31,190
Now, for businesses looking to grow, especially smaller ones, Phrase 17, scalable, is huge.

1072
01:00:31,570 --> 01:00:32,720
It's a word investors love.

1073
01:00:32,750 --> 01:00:42,040
Scalable refers to the ability of a business model, a system, or an idea to expand its operations, grow its customer base, or increase revenue efficiently.

1074
01:00:42,740 --> 01:00:49,840
And this is key while maintaining or even increasing profitability without a proportional jump in costs or effort.

1075
01:00:50,140 --> 01:00:51,850
So growth without costs exploding.

1076
01:00:52,090 --> 01:00:52,690
Exactly.

1077
01:00:52,870 --> 01:00:56,470
It signifies potential for efficient, maybe even exponential growth.

1078
01:00:57,450 --> 01:00:59,180
Think about teaching English.

1079
01:00:59,390 --> 01:01:07,050
In a physical classroom, adding students means more space, more teacher time costs rise proportionally, not very scalable.

1080
01:01:07,150 --> 01:01:07,490
Right.

1081
01:01:07,510 --> 01:01:08,110
Limited seats.

1082
01:01:08,430 --> 01:01:10,810
But teaching English on YouTube, highly scalable.

1083
01:01:10,830 --> 01:01:14,720
You can reach millions, but the cost of your digital classroom doesn't rise nearly as much.

1084
01:01:15,220 --> 01:01:16,890
Profit margins can grow hugely.

1085
01:01:16,930 --> 01:01:21,320
What people sometimes miss isn't just growth, but sustainable, efficient growth.

1086
01:01:21,300 --> 01:01:23,200
That YouTube example makes it super clear.

1087
01:01:23,600 --> 01:01:24,660
Leverage.

1088
01:01:24,680 --> 01:01:31,410
So you might say, this startup, though small now, is inherently scalable due to its digital model and has huge growth potential.

1089
01:01:31,430 --> 01:01:36,940
Or, showing success, she proved her idea was scalable and it brought in way more profit without needing a ton more overhead.

1090
01:01:37,010 --> 01:01:42,780
It's a critical concept today, especially in tech, online businesses, anything venture capital looks at.

1091
01:01:43,700 --> 01:01:51,510
Being able to grow reach and revenue without resources needing to grow at the same rate, that's often the key to rapid, sustainable, profitable growth.

1092
01:01:52,050 --> 01:01:57,060
Okay, let's talk about staying focused, which can be tough and long meetings or complex projects.

1093
01:01:57,780 --> 01:02:00,990
Our next phrase, 18, nails this challenge.

1094
01:02:02,070 --> 01:02:30,320
the weeds or lost in the weeds makes you picture getting tangled up right losing the path that's exactly the visual and the problem being in the weeds means someone or a group has gotten way too focused on tiny unimportant or side details and they've lost sight of the big picture the main goal happens all the time in meetings all the time it's a common trap people get sidetracked by minutiae that distract from the main point it's often a sign the discussion needs pulling back

1095
01:02:30,400 --> 01:02:34,840
So in a meeting going off track, someone might gently say, let's try not to get lost in the weeds here, folks.

1096
01:02:35,110 --> 01:02:37,210
We need to stay focused on the main strategic goal.

1097
01:02:37,390 --> 01:02:37,650
Right.

1098
01:02:37,950 --> 01:02:38,590
Re-centering.

1099
01:02:38,950 --> 01:02:44,560
Or if it's getting too technical, okay, everyone, let's not get lost in the weeds on that specific detail right now.

1100
01:02:44,960 --> 01:02:45,920
We can park that for later.

1101
01:02:46,280 --> 01:02:48,320
Let's bring it back to the overall milestones.

1102
01:02:48,490 --> 01:02:55,240
It's a really effective management tool to guide discussions back to what matters, keep meetings productive, maintain momentum.

1103
01:02:55,860 --> 01:03:02,370
It's about making sure time and energy are spent on the critical path, not bogged down in minor stuff too early.

1104
01:03:02,870 --> 01:03:05,290
Key phrase for keeping communication focused.

1105
01:03:05,950 --> 01:03:06,550
Okay.

1106
01:03:06,570 --> 01:03:14,010
Closely related to staying focused but also managing the conversation flow is phrase 19, to circle back.

1107
01:03:14,030 --> 01:03:16,790
The circle visual really helps returning to a point later.

1108
01:03:16,840 --> 01:03:17,140
Yes.

1109
01:03:17,580 --> 01:03:23,870
To circle back means consciously noting something to return to, reconsider, or discuss later when it's more appropriate.

1110
01:03:24,590 --> 01:03:30,660
It's used a lot when a discussion goes off on a tangent, but the point raised is still valid and needs attention after the current topic is finished.

1111
01:03:30,740 --> 01:03:32,290
So it's like putting it on hold politely.

1112
01:03:32,570 --> 01:03:33,030
Exactly.

1113
01:03:33,110 --> 01:03:36,060
It's a polite, effective way to table something without dismissing it.

1114
01:03:36,360 --> 01:03:37,700
Shows good meeting management.

1115
01:03:37,860 --> 01:03:46,040
So during a meeting, if someone raises a good but off-topic point, you might say, okay, valuable point, but let's circle back to that later in the meeting.

1116
01:03:46,360 --> 01:03:48,860
Want to make sure we finish this agenda item first.

1117
01:03:49,380 --> 01:03:49,700
Perfect.

1118
01:03:50,180 --> 01:03:52,460
Or reminding someone about something from before.

1119
01:03:52,820 --> 01:03:57,190
To circle back to my question earlier about the budget, when do we need that finalized?

1120
01:03:57,170 --> 01:04:08,580
It really highlights its politeness and effectiveness in managing agendas, ensures all relevant topics get covered without derailing the current discussion, allows for thoroughness without losing efficiency.

1121
01:04:09,220 --> 01:04:12,230
It's a hallmark of good time management and collaborative work.

1122
01:04:12,850 --> 01:04:17,130
And our last essential phrase, number 20, is to put a pin in it.

1123
01:04:17,820 --> 01:04:21,760
Feels very similar to circle back, like making a mental note or bookmark.

1124
01:04:21,980 --> 01:04:23,140
It is very similar, yeah.

1125
01:04:23,540 --> 01:04:28,440
Often used interchangeably, though, maybe put a pin in it feels a bit more decisive about pausing it.

1126
01:04:29,050 --> 01:04:33,350
It means acknowledging an important idea or question, but deciding to discuss it later.

1127
01:04:33,390 --> 01:04:34,270
Why later?

1128
01:04:34,430 --> 01:04:38,050
Maybe time constraints, maybe it's off topic for now, maybe you need more info first.

1129
01:04:38,360 --> 01:04:43,560
It's like putting a mental bookmark or even a real note to revisit it later so the idea isn't lost.

1130
01:04:43,540 --> 01:04:48,490
So if a great idea pops up, but it's not for this meeting, you might say, excellent point.

1131
01:04:48,910 --> 01:04:52,610
Let's definitely put a pin in that and we'll tackle it in next week's strategy session.

1132
01:04:53,330 --> 01:04:55,470
Diplomatic way to acknowledge but defer.

1133
01:04:56,390 --> 01:05:00,600
It reinforces its usefulness in managing discussions, especially when time is tight.

1134
01:05:01,020 --> 01:05:06,140
It ensures good ideas aren't lost just because they don't fit the immediate agenda while keeping the current meeting focused.

1135
01:05:06,520 --> 01:05:10,890
Polite, practical, keeps things moving purposefully without losing valuable input.

1136
01:05:11,350 --> 01:05:13,230
Efficient communication embodied.

1137
01:05:13,210 --> 01:05:13,690
Wow.

1138
01:05:13,910 --> 01:05:14,150
Okay.

1139
01:05:14,190 --> 01:05:15,170
20 phrases covered.

1140
01:05:15,210 --> 01:05:16,210
We really dug into those.

1141
01:05:16,470 --> 01:05:17,840
But now let's switch to the shorthand.

1142
01:05:18,580 --> 01:05:19,720
Essential acronyms.

1143
01:05:19,740 --> 01:05:23,450
These are like the fast food of business language built for speed, especially in writing.

1144
01:05:23,970 --> 01:05:26,750
First one, EOD, end of day.

1145
01:05:26,870 --> 01:05:28,510
Why not just type end of day?

1146
01:05:28,830 --> 01:05:30,360
What's the big push for the acronym?

1147
01:05:30,450 --> 01:05:31,480
That's a good question.

1148
01:05:31,940 --> 01:05:34,580
It really boils down to pure efficiency, mostly in writing.

1149
01:05:35,260 --> 01:05:38,280
EOD is almost always written, rarely spoken as EOD.

1150
01:05:38,570 --> 01:05:39,410
You'd say end of day.

1151
01:05:39,710 --> 01:05:44,250
It just means a deadline by the close of the business day, usually 5 p.m. local time, give or take.

1152
01:05:44,510 --> 01:05:52,980
It's a super quick, understood way to set a clear deadline in emails, project tools, short reports, saves typing, saves reading time.

1153
01:05:53,480 --> 01:05:57,730
Speed and conciseness are the drivers, especially with lots of messages flying around.

1154
01:05:57,780 --> 01:06:01,380
So you'd see in an email like, need this report submitted by EOD Wednesday.

1155
01:06:01,820 --> 01:06:04,380
Short, clear, no confusion about the deadline.

1156
01:06:04,400 --> 01:06:04,800
Exactly.

1157
01:06:04,860 --> 01:06:11,190
Its main job is to streamline communication, make emails and reports faster to write and read when deadlines are involved.

1158
01:06:11,570 --> 01:06:14,590
It shows how workplaces adapt language for efficiency.

1159
01:06:15,070 --> 01:06:18,070
Knowing EOD signals you understand common deadlines.

1160
01:06:18,160 --> 01:06:18,740
Acronym two.

1161
01:06:19,040 --> 01:06:20,880
Out of office.

1162
01:06:20,900 --> 01:06:22,260
Another one that's obvious once you know it.

1163
01:06:22,380 --> 01:06:23,820
But again, why abbreviate this?

1164
01:06:24,020 --> 01:06:25,300
Seems like minimal savings.

1165
01:06:25,400 --> 01:06:28,300
Again, it's efficiency, but specifically in automated writing.

1166
01:06:28,480 --> 01:06:33,430
OOO is almost exclusively found in automatic email replies to those out-of-office responders.

1167
01:06:33,650 --> 01:06:34,370
The auto-replies.

1168
01:06:34,670 --> 01:06:35,090
Right.

1169
01:06:35,110 --> 01:06:41,400
It's an instant clear signal someone's away vacation, sick, whatever, and won't be replying immediately.

1170
01:06:41,580 --> 01:06:43,730
You'd almost never say, I'm OOO next week.

1171
01:06:43,850 --> 01:06:45,470
You'd say, I'm out of the office.

1172
01:06:45,490 --> 01:06:48,030
Its power is in being automatic and instant.

1173
01:06:48,010 --> 01:06:52,140
So when you email someone on vacation, you get that immediate bounce back.

1174
01:06:52,160 --> 01:06:53,060
Thanks for your email.

1175
01:06:53,260 --> 01:06:54,940
I am 0-0-0 until Feb 5th.

1176
01:06:55,520 --> 01:06:57,380
Sets the expectation right away.

1177
01:06:57,520 --> 01:06:58,060
Exactly.

1178
01:06:58,400 --> 01:07:01,170
Its whole purpose is setting expectations for response times.

1179
01:07:01,270 --> 01:07:04,310
Tells the sender immediately, don't expect a reply soon.

1180
01:07:04,370 --> 01:07:05,870
Prevents frustration.

1181
01:07:05,930 --> 01:07:08,340
Helps manage communication when someone's away.

1182
01:07:08,390 --> 01:07:10,480
Lets the sender find someone else if it's urgent.

1183
01:07:10,860 --> 01:07:13,780
Basic tool for managing communication during absences.

1184
01:07:13,880 --> 01:07:15,760
Okay, number three is one you hear constantly.

1185
01:07:15,940 --> 01:07:17,120
Inside and outside work.

1186
01:07:17,590 --> 01:07:20,020
ASAP, as soon as possible.

1187
01:07:20,540 --> 01:07:23,140
This one feels strong, urgent, like it adds pressure.

1188
01:07:23,400 --> 01:07:24,420
It absolutely does.

1189
01:07:24,640 --> 01:07:27,270
ASAP means immediately, with utmost urgency.

1190
01:07:27,670 --> 01:07:28,350
It tacks a punch.

1191
01:07:28,730 --> 01:07:33,380
And unlike EOD or OO, this one's used all the time, spoken and written.

1192
01:07:33,400 --> 01:07:34,080
Yeah, you hear it a lot.

1193
01:07:34,100 --> 01:07:36,020
It's a direct call for quick action.

1194
01:07:36,380 --> 01:07:38,240
Signals something is critical, no delays.

1195
01:07:38,550 --> 01:07:40,870
What's interesting is how much power is in those four letters.

1196
01:07:40,970 --> 01:07:42,650
It communicates urgency instantly.

1197
01:07:42,670 --> 01:07:45,260
Overuse it, and it loses its power.

1198
01:07:45,640 --> 01:07:49,530
You get ASA fatigue, like the boy who cried wolf.

1199
01:07:49,550 --> 01:07:51,430
People might ignore it if everything is ASA.

1200
01:07:52,110 --> 01:07:52,690
Good point.

1201
01:07:53,490 --> 01:07:57,540
So on a call, can you send those figures over ASAP?

1202
01:07:57,900 --> 01:08:02,560
Clients waiting, or written, maybe all caps, need your feedback ASAP.

1203
01:08:03,000 --> 01:08:04,430
Definitely signals importance.

1204
01:08:04,470 --> 01:08:10,430
which is why you need to use it carefully, strategically, only when something is genuinely critical and time sensitive.

1205
01:08:11,350 --> 01:08:14,200
Using ASAP wisely shows you understand priority.

1206
01:08:14,540 --> 01:08:18,030
Overusing it can make you seem disorganized or panicky, ironically.

1207
01:08:18,260 --> 01:08:20,320
Okay, acronym four, FYI.

1208
01:08:20,760 --> 01:08:21,640
For your information.

1209
01:08:21,970 --> 01:08:22,910
This one's everywhere.

1210
01:08:23,010 --> 01:08:25,490
People use it casually all the time, maybe without even thinking.

1211
01:08:25,510 --> 01:08:26,270
You're totally right.

1212
01:08:26,470 --> 01:08:27,830
FYI is super common.

1213
01:08:28,370 --> 01:08:34,520
It's used to quickly add a bit of extra info, a just-so-you-know heads-up about something relevant, but that might not need any action or reply.

1214
01:08:34,600 --> 01:08:36,240
So informational, not actionable.

1215
01:08:36,340 --> 01:08:37,140
Mostly, yeah.

1216
01:08:37,160 --> 01:08:41,490
It's way more common in writing emails especially, but you do hear it spoken sometimes, informally.

1217
01:08:41,670 --> 01:08:43,310
Oh, FYI, the meeting room changed.

1218
01:08:43,730 --> 01:08:44,630
It's just a quick flag.

1219
01:08:44,930 --> 01:08:46,920
This is useful info, but you don't need to do anything.

1220
01:08:46,960 --> 01:08:56,090
So in an email update, FYI, the team meeting time next week is now 1.30 p.m. Or casually, oh, FYI, I can't make happy hour tomorrow.

1221
01:08:56,530 --> 01:08:57,260
Quick info dump.

1222
01:08:57,400 --> 01:08:58,160
Exactly.

1223
01:08:58,200 --> 01:09:02,480
Super useful for sharing supplementary info quickly without creating a task.

1224
01:09:02,860 --> 01:09:07,330
Keeps people informed, helps transparency, even if they don't need to act on it right away.

1225
01:09:08,050 --> 01:09:14,150
Knowing when to use FYI helpful but not critical, no reply needed is part of effective communications.

1226
01:09:14,340 --> 01:09:18,310
And our final acronym, number five, a universal sigh of relief.

1227
01:09:18,810 --> 01:09:20,070
TGIF.

1228
01:09:20,740 --> 01:09:22,080
Thank God goodness it's Friday.

1229
01:09:22,660 --> 01:09:24,550
Captures that end of week joy perfectly.

1230
01:09:24,560 --> 01:09:25,240
Absolutely.

1231
01:09:25,560 --> 01:09:28,710
TGIF is that shared feeling of relief looking forward to the weekend.

1232
01:09:28,730 --> 01:09:32,670
You see it written all the time in company chats, Slack, casual emails on Friday afternoon.

1233
01:09:32,690 --> 01:09:33,730
Yeah, like a digital high five.

1234
01:09:33,810 --> 01:09:34,170
Exactly.

1235
01:09:34,190 --> 01:09:35,250
It's a collective cheer.

1236
01:09:35,430 --> 01:09:38,580
Less common spoken formally, but you might hear it casually among colleagues.

1237
01:09:38,640 --> 01:09:40,560
TGIF, have a good weekend.

1238
01:09:40,580 --> 01:09:43,880
And thinking bigger picture, it's a little window into the human side of work.

1239
01:09:44,280 --> 01:09:49,290
Acknowledges the need for balance, the shared effort, the joy of a break, builds connection.

1240
01:09:49,330 --> 01:09:53,440
It really does add to a positive culture, that shared feeling, looking forward to downtime.

1241
01:09:53,850 --> 01:10:00,180
It's a little thing, but it echoes through offices and chats every Friday, builds that team spirit in a small way.

